::CW_U03_L02_GP_Q01::Passage A: Rina gave her last pencil to a classmate before the exam. Passage B: Omar shared his umbrella even though his own sleeve got soaked. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. School supplies and umbrellas are the same object.#Incorrect. The objects differ; the theme connection is generosity.
~B. Weather is always more important than school.#Incorrect. Only Passage B includes weather.
=C. Helping others can require giving up personal comfort or convenience.#Correct. Both characters sacrifice something to help another person.
~D. Exams should happen in the rain.#Incorrect. This combines details in a way the passages do not support.
}
::CW_U03_L02_GP_Q02::Passage A: Mina apologized after blaming her brother for the spill. Passage B: Carter returned the borrowed book after pretending he had lost it. How are the themes similar?{
=A. Both show that honesty can repair damage after a mistake.#Correct. Both characters admit or correct a dishonest action.
~B. Both are mainly about cleaning.#Incorrect. Only Passage A includes a spill, and cleaning is not the theme.
~C. Both show that books are dangerous.#Incorrect. The book is part of one conflict, not the shared theme.
~D. Both prove apologies are useless.#Incorrect. The passages show movement toward repair.
}
::CW_U03_L02_GP_Q03::Passage A: Eli practiced alone until he could play the song. Passage B: Jada asked three classmates for help before she understood the dance steps. Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Both passages are identical because they include names.#Incorrect. Names are surface details, not theme development.
~B. Passage A has music, so Passage B has no theme.#Incorrect. Different activities can still develop related themes.
~C. Passage B is only about three classmates.#Incorrect. The classmates help develop the theme of learning with support.
=D. Both passages show learning through effort, but one emphasizes independent practice while the other emphasizes help from others.#Correct. This compares the shared theme and different development.
}
::CW_U03_L02_GP_Q04::Passage A: The captain admitted the team lost because of his risky play. Passage B: The mayor admitted the town plan had failed and invited everyone to rebuild it. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. Sports and towns are never connected.#Incorrect. Different settings can still develop a related theme.
=B. Leadership includes taking responsibility for mistakes.#Correct. Both leaders admit failure instead of hiding it.
~C. Risky plays always destroy towns.#Incorrect. This inaccurately combines details.
~D. Only captains can be honest.#Incorrect. The mayor is also honest.
}
::CW_U03_L02_GP_Q05::Passage A: A child plants a tree in memory of her grandmother. Passage B: A traveler carries an old recipe card and cooks from it in each new city. What comparison best explains theme development?{
~A. Both passages are only about food.#Incorrect. Passage A is not about food.
~B. Both passages show that moving is impossible.#Incorrect. Passage B involves travel.
=C. Both passages show memory through an object or action that continues after loss.#Correct. The tree and recipe card both turn memory into ongoing action.
~D. Both passages have no universal idea.#Incorrect. Both explore memory and connection.
}
